#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ot-tree-fork SRC DST [--host H] [--jobs N] [--materialize] [--dry]      (FLOW-TREEFORK-R1, FABLE-FLOW-2000 A4/A5)
ot-tree-fork --verify DST [--src SRC]

The one way to get a PRIVATE WRITABLE copy of a die / event tree.  Lanes copied trees by hand (cp -al, cp -a, rsync); on 11-21 a `cp -al` of a
path that had become a symlink to the KV D27d3 event tree made the lane's "private" tree a link into the event tree, its compose wrote
plan_c.json there, and the D27d3 seal verify --ro then failed on 324,773 files with nlink 11 (the tree itself was composed by cp -al).

What a fork is:
  * every regular file is a NEW inode (reflink clone where the filesystem supports FICLONE, else a parallel plain copy): nlink 1, so no write
    or chmod in the fork ever reaches another tree, and the fork never raises the link count of a sealed tree;
  * SRC is resolved once (realpath); nothing that points OUTSIDE SRC is dereferenced: a symlink inside SRC whose target is inside SRC is
    recreated (absolute targets are rewritten to the same place inside DST), a symlink to anything else is kept verbatim, EXCEPT a symlink
    that resolves into an event tree (a kw45 tree, a read-only src_manifest tree, $OT_EVENT_TREE_ROOTS): that is refused (exit 3), because a
    write through it lands in the event tree.  --materialize copies such a target's content into the fork instead (real copy, same rules);
  * DST must not exist (missing parent dirs are made) and must not lie inside SRC, inside an event tree, or behind a symlink into one;
  * the copy is built in DST.forking-<pid> and renamed to DST only after the check: no regular file with nlink > 1
    (`find DST -type f -links +1` = 0; directories always have nlink >= 2, so -type f) and no symlink into an event tree or back into SRC;
  * DST/.fork.json records provenance: src, its realpath, host, src manifest hash / main sha / sealed flag, lane ($OT_LANE), time, method,
    file and byte counts, kept external links.  One line per fork is appended to ~/.local/state/ot-tree-fork/forks.jsonl (src_manifest
    verify reads it to name forks of a broken tree).
  * free space: a plain copy needs SRC bytes + $OT_TREE_FORK_FLOOR_GB (default 50) free on DST's disk, else refused (exit 5).
  * --host H runs the same fork on host H (both paths on H) over ssh; this script is piped, no rt needed there.
  * COPY SLOTS + REUSE (FLOW-FLEET-R1 11-22, FABLE-FLOW-2200 A4: six raw copies at once, epyc10 docker 14 s behind the disk): a plain
    (non-reflink) copy and every cross-host fetch hold one of $OT_TREE_COPY_SLOTS (default 2) flock slots in /var/tmp/ot-tree-copy on the host
    that is written, the same slots src_manifest replicate takes, so a third copy waits instead of piling on the disk.
  * --src-host S (SRC lives on S, DST on --host H or here): when SRC is a manifest tree, H is first asked for a verified read-only tree with
    the same manifest hash (~/.local/state/ot-tree-copy/trees.jsonl, re-checked); one found is forked LOCALLY (nothing over the network).
    Else the tree is fetched once (tar over ssh, under a slot and a per-hash lock, so two lanes asking for the same tree fetch it once) into
    <scratch>/claude/tree-cache/<hash16>, its files checked against the manifest sha256s, made read-only and indexed; then DST is forked from
    that cache.  A SRC without a manifest is fetched straight into DST.forking-<pid> (slot held) and checked like any fork.
  * PLAN LINKS (FLOW-FLEET-R1 11-22, COORD class fix: a launch-tree copy had physical/die_plan as a symlink into the shared famqc tree,
    its bind's R8 refresh rewrote the shared table): a symlink at physical, physical/die_plan or physical/die_plan/<die> (or below) whose
    target lies outside SRC is refused like an event link, event tree or not; --materialize copies its content in (a real private plan).
A failed fork leaves DST.forking-<pid> (printed); delete it with ot-delete.  Exit: 0 ok, 2 usage, 3 refused (event link / bad DST),
4 check failed, 5 no room.  Stdlib only.
"""
import argparse
import concurrent.futures as CF
import errno
